  [21] Consider a regular $n$-gon $P = A_1A_2\ldots A_n$. We need to find all positive integers $n$ such that for any permutation $\sigma(1), \sigma(2), \ldots, \sigma(n)$, there exist indices $1 \le i, j, k \le n$ for which the triangles $A_iA_jA_k$ and $A_{\sigma(i)}A_{\sigma(j)}A_{\sigma(k)}$ are either both acute, both right, or both obtuse.

First, consider a regular $2n$-gon for $n \ge 2$. Let $A_i$ and $A_j$ be diametrically opposite vertices. If $A_{\sigma(i)}$ and $A_{\sigma(j)}$ are also diametrically opposite, any third vertex $A_k$ will satisfy $\angle A_iA_kA_j = 90^\circ = \angle A_{\sigma(i)}A_{\sigma(k)}A_{\sigma(j)}$. Otherwise, let $A_k$ be such that $A_{\sigma(k)}$ is diametrically opposite to $A_{\sigma(i)}$. Then $\angle A_iA_kA_j = 90^\circ = \angle A_{\sigma(i)}A_{\sigma(j)}A_{\sigma(k)}$. This holds for equilateral triangles but fails for regular pentagons.

