**Proof.** Suppose \( f(a) = f(b) = t \). Then,
$$
f(f(a) + y) \mid a + f(y) \quad \text{and} \quad f(f(b) + y) \mid b + f(y).
$$
Since \( f(a) = f(b) \), we have \( f(f(a) + y) = f(f(b) + y) \). Thus,
$$
f(f(a) + y) \mid a + f(y) \quad \text{and} \quad f(f(a) + y) \mid b + f(y).
$$
Therefore,
$$
f(f(a) + y) \mid (a + f(y)) - (b + f(y)) = a - b.
$$
Since \( f(f(a) + y) \) divides \( a - b \) for all \( y \), and \( f(f(a) + y) \) is positive, \( a - b \) must be zero for all \( y \), implying \( a = b \). Hence, \( f \) is injective.
