**Claim.** \( f(1) = 1 \).
**Proof.** Let \( f(1) = t \). Then,
\[ f(t + y) \mid (1 + f(y)) \implies f(y + t) \leq f(y) + 1. \]
Since \( f \) is injective, \( f(1) = 1 \).

**Claim.** \( f(x) = x \) for all \( x \).
**Proof.** Assume \( f(x) \neq x \) for some \( x \). Let \( f(x) = n \) where \( n \neq x \). Then,
\[ f(n + y) \mid (x + f(y)) \implies f(n + y) \leq x + f(y). \]
For \( y = 1 \), \( f(n + 1) \leq x + 1 \). Since \( f \) is injective, \( n + 1 \leq x \), contradicting \( n < x \). Thus, \( f(x) = x \).

