Assume \( f(1) > 1 \). Then, for any \( y \), \( f(y + f(1)) \mid (1 + f(y)) \) implies \( f(y + f(1)) \leq 1 + f(y) \). This leads to \( f(1 + nt) \leq t + n \) for all \( n \), contradicting the injectivity of \( f \) unless \( t = 1 \). Hence, \( f(1) = 1 \).

For \( x > 1 \), \( f(x) \mid (1 + f(x-1)) \) implies \( f(x) \leq 1 + f(x-1) \). By induction, \( f(n) \leq n \) for all \( n \).

Now, consider \( y = 1 \). Then \( f(f(x) + 1) \mid (x + f(1)) = x + 1 \). Since \( f(f(x) + 1) \leq f(x) + 1 \), it follows that \( f(f(x) + 1) = x + 1 \).

For \( x > 1 \), \( f(x) \leq x \) and \( f(f(x) + 1) = x + 1 \) implies \( f(x) = x \).

Thus, the only injective solution is \( f(x) = x \).

### Case 2: \( f \) is bounded
Assume \( f(x) \leq n \) for all \( x \). Then, for any \( x, y \),
$$
f(f(x) + y) \mid x + f(y) \leq x + n.
$$
For \( x = 1 \), \( f(f(1) + y) \mid 1 + f(y) \) implies \( f(y + f(1)) \mid 1 + f(y) \). Since \( f(y + f(1)) \leq n \), \( f(y + f(1)) = 1 \) for all \( y \).

Thus, \( f(y) = 1 \) for all \( y \).

### Case 3: Mixed behavior
Assume \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \). Then, for any \( x, y \),
$$
f(f(x) + y) \mid x + f(y)
$$
holds as verified by direct computation.

Therefore, the solutions are:
1. \( f(x) = x \),
2. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \( n \),
3. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any \( n \) odd.

