Using strong induction, assume \( f(x) = x \) for \( x = 1, \dots, n - 1 \). For \( (x, y) = (n, 1) \), \( f(f(n) + 1) \mid n + 1 \) implies \( f(n) = n \), completing the induction.

**Case 2: \( f \) is bounded.**
Let \( S \) be the set of values in \(\text{img} f\) with infinite preimage. For \( f(a) = f(b) \), \( N = \text{lcm}_{s \in S} s \mid (a - b) \) implies \( |S| \geq N \leq |S| \), so \( S = \{1\} \) or \( \{1, 2\} \).

