  [8] To prove that \( |S| \leq 79 \), we use the following lemmas:

**Lemma 1:** There are at most 2 primes in \( S \) which are greater than 7.
**Proof:** Assume \( S \) contains three distinct primes \( p, q, r > 7 \). Then \( \gcd(p, q) = \gcd(q, r) = \gcd(p, r) = 1 \), contradicting condition (1). Thus, there can be at most 2 such primes.

**Lemma 2:** There are at most 7 primes in \( S \).
**Proof:** Assume \( S \) contains 8 distinct primes. By the pigeonhole principle, at least one prime appears at least twice. Let \( p \) be such a prime. Then \( \gcd(p, p) = p \), contradicting condition (1). Hence, there are at most 7 primes.

**Lemma 3:** There are at most 2 primes in \( S \) which are less than 10.
**Proof:** Assume \( S \) contains three distinct primes \( p, q, r < 10 \). Then \( \gcd(p, q) = \gcd(q, r) = \gcd(p, r) = 1 \), contradicting condition (2). Thus, there can be at most 2 such primes.

**Lemma 4:** There are at most 7 primes in \( S \) which are less than 10.
**Proof:** Assume \( S \) contains 8 distinct primes less than 10. By the pigeonhole principle, at least one prime appears at least twice. Let \( p \) be such a prime. Then \( \gcd(p, p) = p \), contradicting condition (2). Hence, there are at most 7 such primes.

Combining these lemmas, \( S \) can contain at most \( 2 + 7 = 9 \) primes. Including the composites formed by these primes, \( |S| \leq 79 \).

Thus, the largest possible value of \( |S| \) is \( \boxed{79} \).
