f(x) = \sum_{t=0}^{2n} H(n, t) \left| tx - 1 \right|.
\]
To minimize \( f(x) \), we need to find the optimal \( x \). By the properties of the absolute value function and the symmetry of \( H(n, t) \), the minimum occurs when \( x = \frac{1}{n+1} \). This is because the function \( \left| tx - 1 \right| \) is minimized when \( tx \) is as close to 1 as possible, which is achieved when \( x = \frac{1}{n+1} \).

Thus, the \( n \)-tuple that minimizes \( f(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) \) is
\[
\boxed{\left( \frac{1}{n+1}, \frac{1}{n+1}, \ldots, \frac{1}{n+1} \right)}.
\]
