$$
If we pick \( n \equiv 1 \pmod{2} \), then \( k_n \equiv p_n - n \equiv 0 \pmod{2} \) as \( p_n \) is odd, and therefore \( p_n \mid (k_n - 1)! - 1 \).
Since \( k_n > C \) for all sufficiently large \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) for any \( C \in \mathbb{N} \), and considering the pigeonhole principle, we conclude that \( p_n = (k_n - 1)! - 1 \). Hence, \( p_n = n! - 1 \) implies \( p_n - n - 1 = k_n - 1 = n \), which means \( p_n = 2n + 1 \).

