  [8] The polynomial \( f(x) = x^n + ax^{n-1} + pq \) can be factored into two integral polynomials if and only if it has an integer root. By the Rational Root Theorem, any integer root \( r \) must divide \( pq \). Since \( p \) is prime and \( p \nmid q \), the possible integer roots are \( \pm 1 \) and \( \pm p, \pm q \). However, \( \pm p \) and \( \pm q \) cannot be roots because they would make \( r^n + ar^{n-1} \) divisible by \( p \) or \( q \), contradicting the irreducibility condition. Thus, the only possible integer roots are \( 1 \) and \( -1 \).

If \( 1 \) is a root, then:
\[
1^n + a \cdot 1^{n-1} + pq = 0 \implies 1 + a + pq = 0 \implies a = -1 - pq.
\]

If \( -1 \) is a root, then:
\[
(-1)^n + a \cdot (-1)^{n-1} + pq = 0.
\]
For \( n \geq 3 \), \( (-1)^n = 1 \) and \( (-1)^{n-1} = -1 \), so:
\[
1 - a + pq = 0 \implies a = 1 + pq.
\]

Therefore, the polynomial \( x^n + ax^{n-1} + pq \) can be factored into two integral polynomials if and only if \( a = -1 - pq \) or \( a = 1 + pq \).

\[
\boxed{a = -1 - pq \text{ or } a = 1 + pq}
\]

