The size of this subset \( A \) is \( n + 1 \), as it contains all possible values of \( k \) from 0 to \( n \).

Therefore, the largest possible size of a proper subset \( A \) of \( X \) such that for any \( s, t \in A \), both \( s \vee t \) and \( s \wedge t \) are in \( A \) is \( n + 1 \).

