### Lemma 2
For every tapir \(ABCD\), \((A, C)\) and \((B, D)\) are both tasty.
**Proof:** Start with an arbitrary tapir \(ABCD\). Suppose \((A, C)\) was not tasty. If \((A, D)\) is also not tasty, rotating \(A\) away from \(D\) about \(B\) increases \(\angle ABD\) and \(\angle ABC\), preserving \(AB, BC, CD\), while increasing \(AD\). This process must break some condition of the problem, contradicting Lemma 1 or making \(ABCD\) a rhombus of side length 1, contradicting \(AC, BD \leq 1\). \(\blacksquare\)

### Lemma 3
All tapirs have at least one side of length 1.
